    "proved": "Nonnegative curve coefficients give exact ordinary and ideal projections on the separated product locus. After zero coordinates are omitted, the weighted polar metric is complete and geodesic, with closed analytic ideal closure and unique nearest-point ideal projection. Its diameter is twice the sum of the weighted curve areas. Parameter transport is bi-Lipschitz on a common active set, and uniform L1 parameter continuity includes collapse of the entire restricted locus to the divisor potential when all curve coefficients vanish.",
    "mechanism": "Positive curve scaling and descent over inactive factors compute the ordinary base model. Sections over the auxiliary curve and finite-mass countable-divisor domination compute both projections without strict background positivity. Actual scaled rational atomic coefficients give analytic density, and genuine curve restrictions give the converse even when some coefficients vanish. Coordinatewise polar metrics give completeness, ideal distance and transport bounds. Normalized compactness and fiber integration give a uniform L1 estimate. The auxiliary coefficient remains independent of this weighted metric.",
    "retained_boundary": "Only the specified separated curve product locus is covered. No unrestricted higher-dimensional metric or capacity assertion upstairs is claimed. The top-volume comparison expression vanishes, including at the nonbig limit; no big-background theorem is applied there."
